  "worked_solution": "Notice that $f(x,y)$ equals zero when $x=1$ and $y=2.$ These lines divide the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below. Since we have a strict inequality, we use dashed lines to divide the plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052876/assets/2ae1b749a56deaef05254ff376acbd44885337b06b49473cb9b19b4fa57f6c84.png)\n\nTo determine the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region, we pick four test points, one for each region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052876/assets/6434bee1a3dc38292675749072f046d4af6239ce6a8f71f102a9b29637679da4.png)\n\nNext, we calculate the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region using a sign table:\n\n|  | Test point | $(x-1)$ | $(y-2)$ | $f(x,y)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Top-Left | $(0,3)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| Top-Right | $(2,3)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| Bottom-Left | $(0,1)$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| Bottom-Right | $(2,1)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n\n \n\nFinally, on our diagram, we shade in the solutions to $f(x,y)>0$ using the *last* column of the table above.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14052876/assets/17551c745895bac085fc289f9ff0f26531bc518891815e252885fd75d81a0af0.png)",
